<div class="row mb-5">
    <div class="col-lg-12">
        <h1 class="display-4 fw-bold mb-3">Refills &amp; Compliance</h1>
        <p class="lead mb-4">
            Turn chronic scripts into a managed queue — schedule repeats at dispense time, chase
            what is overdue, and process the next pack without rebuilding the prescription from
            scratch.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="row mb-5">
    <div class="col-lg-12">
        <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mb-4">Schedule at the counter</h2>
        <p>
            When filling a script, set repeatable lines with total repeats and interval days.
            BasaRx creates refill schedules tied to the original dispense so subsequent packs
            inherit medicine, directions, and patient context. Optional batch preference keeps
            FEFO discipline consistent across the refill lifecycle.
        </p>
        <p>
            Patients see continuity; pharmacists see a structured worklist instead of sticky notes
            and calendar reminders.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="row mb-5">
    <div class="col-lg-12">
        <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mb-4">Pending, due, and overdue queues</h2>
        <p>
            Filter refills by status — overdue, due today, due soon, or processing. Process a
            refill into a new dispense from the queue or from the patient profile. Cancel or
            reschedule when therapy changes. Status refresh keeps the board honest as days pass.
        </p>
        <p>
            Command Center KPIs surface pending and overdue counts so managers know compliance
            risk before the phone starts ringing.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="row mb-5">
    <div class="col-lg-12">
        <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mb-4">Reminders that reach patients</h2>
        <p>
            Scheduled jobs send email and SMS for due and overdue refills, reducing missed therapy
            without manual dialling lists. Combined with allergy-aware patient profiles and active
            medications views, refill operations stay clinical — not just transactional.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

